WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.080
An average adult human mouth contains
32 teeth.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.640
The agreed definition of a tooth

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.080
is a highly mineralised
unit within the mouth.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.760
Thirty-two highly mineralised units,
eight incisors, four canines,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.160
eight premolars and eight molars.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.280
Plus four wisdom teeth which don’t
tend to make themselves known

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.000
until about five years after the
rest of the adult set are in place,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.240
because of course these 32 teeth
are your second set.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.320
An average baby is born
with both milk and adult teeth

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.280
already in their head.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.560
A seven-year-old Indian boy was
recently found to have 526 teeth

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.800
inside his mouth.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.640
But let's stick to the more common
number. 20 milk teeth, 32 adult,

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.200
a total of 52.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.280
Fifty-two teeth, rarely visible
when born but they're all there,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.160
in the skull.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:42.560
Waiting...

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.080
waiting...

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.000
to erupt!

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.520
Thank goodness we can't remember
teething. Consider if you will the

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.520
brutality of these shards of enamel
ripping through innocent gums.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.160
No wonder babies cry.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.640
Reward for this discomforting
experience comes when that first set

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.080
of baby teeth start to fall out,
ready for the tooth fairy.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.120
The first recording of such a
transaction with a fee being paid

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.200
for a child's lost tooth
is in 13th Century Norse culture.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.760
Vikings going into battle
would wear necklaces

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.240
made up of children's teeth.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.360
Not because it presumably
looked utterly terrifying,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.160
but because they were
considered lucky.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.000
In the UK, the most common
price paid for a tooth is £1,

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.520
although 11% of children
apparently sleep soundly

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.000
with a crisp fiver under their
pillow - far less lumpy than a coin.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.680
Children in the Middle Ages were
sometimes instructed to burn

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.000
their baby teeth, or risk spending
the afterlife searching for them...

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.160
With such historical scaremongering
surrounding teeth, it is perhaps

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.840
easier to understand why so many
people now have a fear of dentists.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.040
Eighty-four per cent of all adults
in the UK have at least one filling

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.720
and the average Brit has seven.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.920
Of course fillings are just
one dental procedure,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.400
your teeth may also be cleaned,
flossed, capped or crowned,

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.680
bonded, braced, sealed,
whitened or extracted.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.920
But just remember, that going to the
dentist today is far less precarious

00:01:59.000 --> 00:01:59.840
than it used to be.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.640
Before dentistry found acceptance
as a medical profession,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.760
extraction was the domain
of barbers and blacksmiths.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.280
Essentially, anyone with access
to sharp tools or heavy pliers.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.400
As 19th Century dentists like
Horace Wells and William Morton

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.280
struggled to be considered
professionals rather than tradesman,

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.680
public demonstrations of painless
tooth extractions became

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.360
more and more commonplace.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.200
Some using nitrous oxide ended in
a volunteer screaming in agony,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.840
but by 1846, dentistry had
finally cracked anaesthesia.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.080
People attempt to please their
dentists by brushing their teeth,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.000
a practice that has evolved from
twigs and leaves, into a global

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.400
toothpaste industry worth billions.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.240
Human teeth are now primarily used
for biting off strips of sticky tape.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.360
But these highly mineralised rocks
are also responsible for crushing,

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.200
slicing and making manageable
almost every meal or snack

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.280
you'll ever enjoy.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.160
But we also use them
to reflect our mood to others,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.240
be that a welcoming smile
or an aggressive snarl.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.920
Contemporary teeth can be
adorned with jewelled grilles

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.760
or filtered to perfection
on Instagram,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.280
but to think of the all-white
uniform smile as perfect

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.160
is a relatively recent obsession.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.120
Having a varied set of teeth is what
allowed humans to survive

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.960
and flourish across the globe.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.280
Our teeth have evolved to incorporate
varying diets and environments,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.160
frankly, we're lucky to have them.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.360
Alligator teeth are considered
particularly lucky.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.840
But if you're seeking dental
inspiration, look no further than...

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.760
sharks.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.000
Unlike us, sharks can afford to loose
a few teeth as they're able

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.800
to regenerate and replace
them almost continuously.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.920
One great white shark can produce
tens of thousands of teeth

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.280
in their lifetime,
which makes our 52 seem relatively...

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.160
toothless.

